1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,640
And I got to experience Palestine, not just as the place that my family was from, I got to experience oppression.

2
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:14,780
Like I remember going to visit my uncle in a prison and someone having to explain to me, oh, well, your uncle was in a group of men and then the IDF came and they detained them all.

3
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:20,560
And then my uncle was in this what they call arbitrary detention where they just could make it up as they go along.

4
00:00:20,820 --> 00:00:25,960
People are not often charged with crimes. And it's like and so even as a young child going to Palestine, I was like, this is not right.

5
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:29,180
And then my mom had to sit me down and be like, let's explain what's going on here.

6
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:32,180
Why are there soldiers here? Why is there an occupation? What happened?

7
00:00:32,260 --> 00:00:35,140
What's the. So I got to learn my lineage and my story.

8
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:40,940
And then I was able to be the person in the U.S. that invoked my Palestinianness, which is what my parents taught us to be.

9
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:45,300
They said, if anybody asks you where you're from, yes, you're born and raised in Brooklyn, but you are Palestinian.

10
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:51,160
And I think that's part of it. That coupled with being the oldest child, that being coupled with being a child of immigrants.

11
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:56,300
I'm always 10 toes down every time, everywhere, every place. You could always count on me in that space.

12
00:00:56,340 --> 00:00:57,840
And that's kind of how I'm known in the movement.

